/// \defgroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping Staging-room copies, ICMP ping, GameSpy callbacks and connection quitting
/// \ingroup grp_network
/// \brief Small functions from the online lobby, its GameSpy and FESL
///        clients and the lockstep connection layer: copying and
///        destroying a GameSpy staging room, measuring an ICMP ping,
///        ordering the lobby's player map, two small functions at the
///        GameSpy SDK boundary (a room-key wrapper and a Presence
///        callback), a FESL echo message, marking a connection as quitting
///        and reading a game's map path.
///
/// These functions do not form one module. Each is small, and each sits on
/// a seam of the network subsystem (\ref sub_network) that a modder or a
/// port will meet:
/// - **GameSpy staging rooms.** A GameSpyStagingRoom is the online
///   lobby's record of one game: the game-setup data that GameInfo shares
///   with the LAN lobby, plus GameSpy data. In Zero Hour that data includes
///   the GameSpy slots, game name and id, password and observer flags,
///   version and CRC values, ladder address, ping string and reported
///   player counts. BFME 2's GameInfo, the part shared with LAN games, also
///   carries a 16-byte digest that Zero Hour's GameInfo and
///   GameSpyStagingRoom both lack: GameInfo::reset clears it, GameInfo's
///   lobby icon reads it, and the LAN lobby sends it in its game
///   announcements.
///   GameSpyInfo keeps the listed games as staging rooms keyed by game id.
///   GameSpyInfo::addStagingRoom receives a room by value and passes it on
///   by value to GameSpyInfo::removeStagingRoom, so a room is copied and
///   destroyed on every add and remove. As in Zero Hour, addStagingRoom
///   first removes any existing entry for the game. Unlike Zero Hour, BFME
///   2's addStagingRoom then returns without keeping the room when the
///   room's digest is not all zeros and a lookup keyed by that digest
///   (printed as hexadecimal text) does not recognise it. Only a room that
///   passes this check is assigned into a newly allocated room, which is
///   the one kept. BFME 2's LAN lobby applies the same kind of check: its
///   game-announcement handler drops a game whose announced digest is not
///   all zeros and is not recognised by the same lookup. What the digest
///   identifies is not settled here: comments in the BFME 2 units read it
///   both as naming a saved game (the LAN handler, and the resumed-save
///   icon GameInfo shows in the lobby) and as naming a map (PeerDefs.cpp).
///   BFME 2's copy constructor and destructor for the class contain only
///   the memberwise sequence a compiler generates; whether the original
///   source declared them is not known. The copy copies the base part,
///   installs the class's vtable, then copies each slot (through the
///   compiler's exception-aware array-copy helper), the strings, which
///   share their text by reference count, and the plain fields. In Zero
///   Hour a copy leaves GameInfo's slot pointers aimed at the source room's
///   slots, which is why addStagingRoom calls
///   GameSpyStagingRoom::cleanUpSlotPointers on the room it keeps; BFME
///   2's addStagingRoom makes the same call.
/// - **Pinging.** MeasureIcmpPing sends one ICMP echo through the Windows
///   ICMP API. In Zero Hour the ping worker threads behind ThePinger call
///   the same code once per repetition and average the results.
/// - **The player map.** AsciiComparator orders GameSpyInfo's map of the
///   players in the current lobby room by nickname, ignoring letter case.
/// - **GameSpy SDK boundary.** dispatchIndexedValue sets chat-channel keys for
///   one of the peer connection's rooms, and getNickForMessage is the
///   GameSpy Presence callback that names the sender of a buddy message.
/// - **FESL.** FeslEchoNotifier::notifyEcho builds and sends one message
///   of EA's FESL online protocol.
/// - **Lockstep.** Connection::setQuitting stores the quit frame (the next
///   logic frame) and the quit time. Once the stored value is no longer
///   -1, ConnectionManager::isPlayerConnected stops counting the player as
///   connected.
/// - **Game setup.** GameInfo::getMap returns the map path that game start,
///   the lobbies, the options string and replays read.
///
/// \par Modding notes
/// - Nothing here reads INI, map scripts, Lua or other game data. The
///   message type, field names and route of the FESL echo, the ICMP request
///   (size, fill pattern and time-to-live) and the case rule of the player
///   map are all compiled in.
/// - Zero Hour's login pings take the list of ping servers, the timeout
///   and the number of repetitions from the GameSpy configuration text it
///   downloads, not from game data. Its disconnect screen also pings the
///   configured servers in turn, but with a compiled-in repetition count
///   and timeout. How BFME 2 feeds its ping requests is not established
///   here.
/// - BFME 2's online game list leaves out a game whose advertised digest
///   is non-zero and not recognised by the client's lookup (see the
///   staging-room bullet above). Because the existing entry is removed
///   before the check, a game already in the list disappears when a later
///   advertisement for it fails the check. The LAN game list behaves the
///   same way: a failing announcement removes the game it names.
/// - Only Connection::setQuitting and GameInfo::getMap touch state that a
///   lockstep game depends on. The quit frame is derived from the logic
///   frame, and the map path selects the map every player loads.
///
/// \par Remastering notes
/// - MeasureIcmpPing is a Win32 seam: it loads ICMP.DLL by name and calls
///   IcmpCreateFile, IcmpSendEcho and IcmpCloseHandle. Other platforms have
///   no such API, and an ICMP echo there usually needs a raw socket with
///   extra privileges or a platform-specific ICMP datagram socket.
/// - Connection::setQuitting stamps time with winmm's timeGetTime, a
///   millisecond clock; a port needs an equivalent monotonic clock.
/// - The GameSpy helpers and the FESL echo follow the wire formats of those
///   services. A replacement back end has to accept them as built here, or
///   a port replaces those client layers as a whole.
///
/// \see \ref sub_network

/// \fn GameSpyStagingRoom::~GameSpyStagingRoom()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief Destroys a staging room: its string members, then each slot,
///        then its base part.
///
/// The destructor is virtual, and the class's deleting destructor calls
/// it, so deleting a room stored in the game list, as
/// GameSpyInfo::removeStagingRoom does, runs it. It also runs on the
/// by-value room parameters of
/// GameSpyInfo::addStagingRoom and GameSpyInfo::removeStagingRoom when
/// those calls return.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// BFME 2's body contains only the memberwise teardown, which matches the
/// compiler-generated sequence: release each narrow-string member (an
/// AsciiString drops its reference to the shared text), destroy each slot
/// through the compiler's array-destructor helper, and run the base
/// destructor. Zero Hour's StagingRoomGameInfo.h declares no destructor
/// for the class; whether BFME 2's original source declared one is not
/// known.
///
/// \see GameSpyStagingRoom::cleanUpSlotPointers,
///      GameSpyInfo::addStagingRoom, GameSpyInfo::removeStagingRoom

/// \fn Int MeasureIcmpPing(UnsignedInt destAddress, Int timeoutMs)
/// \brief Sends one ICMP echo request to an IPv4 address and returns the
///        round-trip time in milliseconds, or -1 when no reply arrives.
///
/// Zero Hour does this in PingThreadClass::doPing. Each ping worker
/// thread takes a request (a host name, a repetition count and a timeout),
/// resolves the host to an address, calls doPing once per repetition, and
/// posts the average of the replies that succeeded, or -1 when none did.
/// After login, Zero Hour turns those averages into the local player's
/// ping string, which the lobby uses to estimate the latency between
/// players. BFME 2's body performs the same steps as Zero Hour's doPing;
/// that BFME 2 uses it the same way is inferred from Zero Hour, not traced
/// from a BFME 2 caller.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Loads ICMP.DLL with LoadLibrary and looks up IcmpCreateFile,
///   IcmpCloseHandle and IcmpSendEcho by name on every call. If the
///   library or any of the three functions is missing, or no ICMP handle
///   can be opened, it returns -1.
/// - Sends 32 bytes of printable ASCII, counting up from the space
///   character, with a time-to-live of 64 and no IP options, and waits up
///   to the timeout for one reply. The request and reply buffers are on
///   the stack and are each several kilobytes, far more than one reply
///   needs.
/// - Reads the round-trip time from the first reply and clamps it to the
///   timeout. Zero Hour's comment says replies sometimes report slightly
///   more than the timeout. The reply's status is not checked: any reply
///   counts as a measurement.
/// - Frees the library before returning.
///
/// \param destAddress The IPv4 address to ping, as Winsock stores it
///                    (network byte order); Zero Hour passes the result of
///                    inet_addr or gethostbyname.
/// \param timeoutMs   How long to wait for the reply, in milliseconds. It
///                    is also the upper bound of the result.
/// \return The round-trip time in milliseconds, at most \p timeoutMs, or -1
///         on any failure.
///
/// \note As in Zero Hour, when IcmpSendEcho returns no reply the function
///       skips IcmpCloseHandle, so the ICMP handle opened for that call is
///       never closed. A port should close it on every path.

/// \fn bool AsciiComparator::operator()(AsciiString s1, AsciiString s2) const
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief Case-insensitive less-than of two narrow strings; the ordering of
///        GameSpyInfo's player map.
///
/// The player map (PlayerInfoMap in Zero Hour's PeerDefs.h) maps an
/// AsciiString to a PlayerInfo and uses this comparator; BFME 2's map code
/// is instantiated with the same three types. GameSpyInfo::updatePlayerInfo
/// stores each PlayerInfo under the player's name, so the map holds the
/// players of the current lobby room by nickname. Its lookup, insertion
/// and operator[] code calls this comparator.
///
/// Because two names that differ only in letter case compare as neither
/// less nor greater, the map treats them as the same key: an update for
/// one overwrites the other's PlayerInfo, while the map keeps the key
/// spelling it stored first.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls the C runtime's _strcmpi on the two texts and returns true when
/// the result is negative. Zero Hour's source spells the call stricmp. Both
/// strings are taken by value, so every comparison copies two AsciiString
/// references and releases them again; a port can take const references
/// without changing the order.
///
/// \param s1 The left-hand name.
/// \param s2 The right-hand name.
/// \return True when \p s1 sorts before \p s2, ignoring case.
///
/// \see GameSpyInfo::updatePlayerInfo

/// \fn void dispatchIndexedValue(void *object, int index, void *user, int num, int *keys, int *values)
/// \brief Sets chat-channel keys for one room of the GameSpy peer
///        connection, unless the object's guard fields rule it out.
///
/// PeerThreadClass::pushStatsToRoom calls it twice, for room 1 and room 2,
/// with the local login name and the arrays of stats keys and values it
/// has just formatted. Another caller in the peer thread sets further keys
/// for the same two rooms; the user argument it passes is not identified.
/// Zero Hour's pushStatsToRoom makes its two matching calls to the GameSpy
/// peer SDK's peerSetRoomKeys, for the group room and the staging room.
/// This function therefore plays that part in BFME 2; whether it is the
/// SDK function under another name is not established.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns without doing anything unless a field of the object is set and
/// at least one of two per-room entries for \p index is non-zero.
/// Otherwise it forwards to the GameSpy chat SDK's chatSetChannelKeysA
/// with the object's chat connection and the channel name the object keeps
/// for that room, passing \p user, \p num, \p keys and \p values through
/// unchanged. The forward is a tail jump that reuses the caller's stack
/// arguments.
///
/// \param object The peer connection object (the PEER handle in the
///               callers).
/// \param index  The room, used to select the room's two entries and its
///               channel name; the traced callers pass 1 and 2.
/// \param user   The nickname whose keys are set; pushStatsToRoom passes
///               the local login name. What the other caller passes is
///               not identified.
/// \param num    The number of key and value pairs.
/// \param keys   The key names, an array of \p num string pointers.
/// \param values The values, an array of \p num string pointers.
///
/// \see PeerThreadClass::pushStatsToRoom

/// \fn void getNickForMessage(GPConnection *, GPGetInfoResponseArg *arg, void *param)
/// \brief GameSpy Presence callback that copies the sender's nickname into
///        a pending buddy-message response.
///
/// BuddyThreadClass::messageCallback handles an incoming buddy message. It
/// asks gpGetInfo for the sender's profile, with the options to use the
/// cache and to block, passing this function and the BuddyResponse it is
/// building. Because the request blocks, this callback has filled in the
/// nickname by the time messageCallback converts the message text and
/// queues the response on TheGameSpyBuddyMessageQueue. BFME 2's
/// messageCallback does this as Zero Hour's does.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the nickname from the profile information into the response's
/// message nickname with the C runtime's _mbscpy, where Zero Hour's source
/// calls strcpy. The GameSpy connection argument is not used.
///
/// \param arg   The profile information the GameSpy SDK returns; only its
///              nickname is read.
/// \param param The BuddyResponse that messageCallback passed to gpGetInfo.
///
/// \warning There is no length check. The copy relies on the nickname
///          fitting the response's nickname field, as in Zero Hour.
///
/// \see BuddyThreadClass::messageCallback

/// \fn void FeslEchoNotifier::notifyEcho()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief Builds one FESL `ECHO` message with the notifier's transaction id
///        and, when it holds them, its UGID and SECRET strings, and sends
///        it on the notifier's connection.
///
/// FeslEchoNotifier belongs to the EA FESL online client code that BFME 2
/// carries and Zero Hour does not. It is part of a larger FESL object: the
/// only caller is a periodic update of that object, whose assertion text
/// names EA's source file gamebrowserdemangler.cpp. While an echo is
/// pending, the update calls this function again on a fixed interval and
/// gives up after a fixed number of attempts. The file name suggests the
/// game browser's NAT traversal ("demangling") code; what the server does
/// with the echo is not established here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The message is built in a stack buffer. Its type code is the four
/// characters `ECHO`, and three header values are copied from a record
/// reached through the notifier's owner pointer. Then the body adds these
/// fields, in order:
/// | Field | Value |
/// |---|---|
/// | `TID` | the notifier's transaction id |
/// | `TYPE` | always 1 |
/// | `UGID` | a string the notifier holds; added only when that string is not empty |
/// | `SECRET` | a second string the notifier holds; added together with `UGID` |
///
/// The message is then handed to the FESL sender with the route string
/// `->D` and the notifier's connection.
///
/// \note BFME 1 has the same function; its body differs from BFME 2's
///       only in immediate operand values.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The type code, field names, the constant `TYPE` value and the route are
/// compiled into this body. A replacement FESL back end has to accept the
/// message as built here.

/// \fn void Connection::setQuitting(UnsignedInt quitFrame)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief Marks a peer connection as quitting by storing a quit frame and
///        the wall-clock time at which that happened.
///
/// A Connection is the lockstep layer's link to one other player.
/// ConnectionManager::processPlayerLeave calls this with the next logic
/// frame (the current frame plus one): for the leaving player's connection,
/// or for every connection when the local player is the one leaving.
/// The connection constructor stores -1 in the same field, and
/// ConnectionManager::isPlayerConnected counts another player as connected
/// only while that player's connection still holds -1 there, so after this
/// call the player no longer counts as connected. That check compares the
/// stored value only with -1; how BFME 2 uses the frame value itself is
/// not traced here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Stores \p quitFrame in the connection's quit-frame field and the current
/// timeGetTime value in its quit-time field.
///
/// \param quitFrame The quit frame; the traced caller passes the next logic
///                  frame. Any value other than -1 (the constructor's
///                  "still connected" marker) makes the player count as
///                  disconnected; any other use of the frame value is not
///                  traced.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's setQuitting takes no argument; it sets a Bool flag and
///       the quit time. BFME 2, like BFME 1, stores a frame number in
///       place of the flag. Zero Hour's Connection::doSend uses the quit
///       time to drop a quitting connection's queued messages once a
///       timeout has passed; how BFME 2 reads it is not traced here.
///
/// \see ConnectionManager::processPlayerLeave,
///      ConnectionManager::isPlayerConnected

/// \fn AsciiString GameInfo::getMap(void) const
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief Returns a copy of the game's map path.
///
/// GameInfo describes a game being set up, for the LAN and GameSpy lobbies
/// and for replays. Callers of this accessor include game start in the
/// logic (populateRandomSideAndColor, populateRandomStartPosition), the
/// setup screens' map preview (positionStartSpots, a Zero Hour menu
/// helper), the LAN lobby (LANAPI::OnGameStart, LANAPI::OnHasMap,
/// LANAPI::RequestHasMap), the game options string (GameInfoToAsciiString,
/// ParseGameOptionsString), the GameSpy lobby
/// (GameSpyInfo::setGameOptions) and replay playback
/// (RecorderClass::playbackFile).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copy-constructs the stored AsciiString into the caller's return value,
/// which shares the text buffer by reference count. Zero Hour defines the
/// accessor inline in GameInfo.h; BFME 2 also has an out-of-line copy,
/// which these callers reach.
///
/// \return The map path. Changing the returned string does not change the
///         game's setup.
///
/// \note Calls also arrive from bridge and terrain-road code
///       (W3DBridge::load, TerrainRoadCollection::newBridge). They are
///       consistent with the retail linker merging other classes' identical
///       one-line string accessors into this body, and have nothing to do
///       with game setup.
///
/// \par Modding
/// GameInfoToAsciiString reads this value when it builds the game options
/// string the host shares with the other players. The stored value is a
/// map file path: when BFME 2's staging room launches a Living World
/// battle, for example, it stores `maps\<name>\<name>.map`, built from
/// the battle's map name. BFME 2, like Zero Hour, converts the stored
/// path to a portable form before writing it (after the map contents mask)
/// into the options string, together with the map's CRC and file size;
/// ParseAsciiStringToGameInfo reads all three back. When a client applies
/// them, GameInfo::setMapCRC looks the map up in the client's own map
/// cache and marks the local player's slot as not having the map when the
/// map is unknown or the cached CRC differs. A map with the same path but
/// different contents therefore does not count as the same map. In Zero
/// Hour, DoAnyMapTransfers then sends the map to players whose slot lacks
/// it; how BFME 2 acts on the flag is not traced here (\ref sub_network).

/// \file GameSpy/Thread/PingThread.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_network_gamespy_ping
/// \brief The ICMP round-trip measurement MeasureIcmpPing (in Zero Hour,
///        the ping worker threads' doPing).
///
/// \see MeasureIcmpPing

